<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Himeji Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1871 CE · capital Himeji</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain centred on Himeji castle, the great white keep that Ikeda Terumasa built and that survives as Japan's finest original castle.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harima-province">Harima</a> to his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-terumasa">Ikeda Terumasa</a> with 520,000 koku in 1600. Between 1601 and 1609 Terumasa rebuilt Himeji castle on the site of an earlier fortress, creating the present complex of white-plastered towers.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Himeji guarded the western approaches to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, so the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> placed trusted families there and moved them often. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honda-tadamasa">Honda Tadamasa</a>, whose wife was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senhime">Senhime</a>, granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>, held it from 1617. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sakai">Sakai</a> family ruled from 1749 to the end, and the reformer Kawai Sundo restored finances through a domain monopoly on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Because its lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakai-tadashige">Sakai Tadashige</a> was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>'s great elder, Himeji was treated as an enemy in 1868 and surrendered when besieged by imperial forces. It became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyogo-prefecture">Hyogo Prefecture</a> in 1871, and the castle became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1993.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/himeji-domain.jpg" alt="Himeji Domain" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Hachigamine · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A7%AB%E8%B7%AF%E8%97%A9%E3%81%AE%E8%BB%8D%E8%89%A6%E6%97%97.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Himeji</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ikeda Terumasa, Honda Tadamasa, Sakai Tadashige</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Himeji han</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=himeji-domain" aria-label="Himeji Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8097 918 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Himeji Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8417 1174L8530 1145M8417 1174L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8417 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8417 1174h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Himeji. <a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=himeji-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
